 Cataract of the Nile, in present-day Sudan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napir-asu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napir-asu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napir-Asu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Napir-Asu was a queen of Elam in the fourteenth century BCE, wife of King Untash-Napirisha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napirisha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napirisha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napirisha</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Napirisha, 'great god', was one of the chief deities of ancient Elam in south-western Iran, worshipped with the goddess Kiririsha and the god Inshushinak of Susa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naplanum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naplanum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naplanum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Naplanum was an Amorite chief traditionally counted as the first king of Larsa in southern Mesopotamia, ruling around 2025 BCE after the decline of the Third Dynasty of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naples"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naples.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naples</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Naples is the largest city of southern Italy, on the Bay of Naples in Campania.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napo-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napo-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napo River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Napo River is a tributary of the Amazon that rises on the slopes of the Ecuadorian Andes and flows south-east through Ecuador and Peru to join the Amazon below…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napoleon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Napoleon Bonaparte was a French military leader who seized power after the French Revolution and ruled as Emperor of the French.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napoleon III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Napoleon III, nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte, was President of France and then Emperor of the French during the Second Empire from 1852 to 1870.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-louis-bonaparte"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-louis-bonaparte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napoléon Louis Bonaparte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Napoléon Louis Bonaparte was the son of Louis Bonaparte, King of Holland, and Hortense de Beauharnais, and the elder brother of the future Napoleon III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napoleonic Code</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Napoleonic Code is the French civil code issued under Napoleon in 1804, codifying law on persons, property and contracts.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Napoleonic Wars</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1803–1815</span><span class="enc-snip">The Napoleonic Wars were a series of wars between Napoleon's France and shifting coalitions of European powers from 1803 to 1815.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naqada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naqada</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Naqada is a town on the west bank of the Nile in Upper Egypt, north of Luxor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Naqsh-e Jahan Square is a large public square in Isfahan, Iran, laid out under Shah Abbas I when he made the city the Safavid capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Naqsh-e Rostam</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Naqsh-e Rostam is an ancient necropolis near Persepolis in the province of Fars, Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/nara"><span class="enc-name">Nara</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Nara is a city in the Kansai region of Japan and the capital of Nara Prefecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/nara-narayan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Nara Narayan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Nara Narayan was a king of the Koch dynasty whose reign, from 1540 to 1587, was the high point of the Koch kingdom in northern Bengal and western Assam.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narada-smriti"><span class="enc-name">Narada Smriti</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Narada Smriti is a Sanskrit legal text in the Dharmashastra tradition.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narai was king of Ayutthaya from 1656 to 1688.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narakasura"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narakasura.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narakasura</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narakasura is a demon king of Hindu legend, said to have ruled Pragjyotisha, the ancient name of Kamarupa in Assam.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin"><span class="enc-name">Naram-Sin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Naram-Sin was a king of the Akkadian Empire who reigned from 2254 to 2218 BCE and was the grandson of Sargon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narapati-i-of-ava"><span class="enc-name">Narapati I of Ava</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narapati I was King of Ava in Upper Burma from 1442 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narapatisithu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narapatisithu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narapatisithu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narapatisithu, also known as Sithu II, was king of Pagan from 1174 to 1211, a reign often seen as the height of the kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasa-nayaka"><span class="enc-name">Narasa Nayaka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasa Nayaka was a general of the Vijayanagara Empire and the father of Krishnadevaraya.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-i-hoysala"><span class="enc-name">Narasimha I (Hoysala)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimha I was king of the Hoysala dynasty from 1152 to 1173, the son and successor of Vishnuvardhana.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-ii-hoysala"><span class="enc-name">Narasimha II (Hoysala)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimha II was king of the Hoysala dynasty from 1220 to 1235, the son of Veera Ballala II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimha-iii-hoysala"><span class="enc-name">Narasimha III (Hoysala)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimha III was king of the Hoysala dynasty who ruled the Karnataka homeland from Dwarasamudra, while his half-brother Ramanatha held the Tamil territories.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narasimhadeva-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narasimhadeva I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimhadeva I was a king of the Eastern Ganga dynasty of Odisha in India, ruling from 1238 to 1264.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narasimhavarman I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimhavarman I, known as Mamalla, 'great wrestler', was king of the Pallava dynasty from about 630 to 668.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narasimhavarman-ii"><span class="enc-name">Narasimhavarman II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narasimhavarman II, also called Rajasimha, was a Pallava king of south India who reigned in a time of relative peace around 700 to 728.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate"><span class="enc-name">Narathihapate</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narathihapate was king of Pagan from 1256 to 1287.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narathu"><span class="enc-name">Narathu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narathu was king of Pagan from 1167 to about 1171, the son and successor of Alaungsithu, whom tradition says he killed.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narayanapala"><span class="enc-name">Narayanapala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narayanapala was a king of the Pala dynasty of Bengal and Bihar who reigned for over fifty years in the second half of the ninth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narayanhiti-palace.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narayanhiti Palace</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Narayanhiti Palace in Kathmandu was the main residence of the kings of Nepal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narbonne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narbonne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narbonne</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Narbonne is a city in the Aude department of southern France, near the Mediterranean coast.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narciso-claveria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narciso Clavería</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narciso Clavería was a Spanish general and Governor-General of the Philippines from 1844 to 1849.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narendra-modi"><span class="enc-name">Narendra Modi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narendra Modi (born 1950) is an Indian politician of the Bharatiya Janata Party who has been prime minister of India since 2014.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narendra-shah"><span class="enc-name">Narendra Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narendra Shah was maharaja of Tehri Garhwal, a princely state in the Himalayas, from 1919 to 1946.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/narendradeva"><span class="enc-name">Narendradeva</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Narendradeva was a Licchavi king of Nepal who regained the throne in the 640s with Tibetan help after a period of exile in Tibet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/narendranagar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/narendranagar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Narendranagar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Narendranagar is a town in the Tehri Garhwal district of Uttarakhand, India, in the hills above Rishikesh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/naresh-chandra-singh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/naresh-chandra-singh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"